1. <div> Tag

The <div> tag is a container used to group HTML elements together.

It is commonly used to create different sections of a webpage and apply CSS styles.

Syntax
<div>
    <h1>Welcome</h1>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
</div>
Example
<div>
    <h2>Student Information</h2>
    <p>Name: Rahul</p>
    <p>Course: Java Full Stack</p>
</div>

Important: <div> is a block-level element, so it normally starts on a new line and takes the available width.

2. Inline CSS

Inline CSS is written directly inside the HTML element using the style attribute.

Syntax
<tag style="property: value;">
Example
<h1 style="color: blue;">Welcome</h1>

<p style="font-size: 20px;">
    This is a paragraph.
</p>
Multiple CSS Properties
<div style="color: white; background-color: blue; padding: 20px;">
    Student Information
</div>

Here:

color → Text color
background-color → Background color
padding → Space inside the element
Advantages
Easy for small changes
Useful for testing styles quickly
Disadvantages
Difficult to maintain
Repeated styles make HTML lengthy
3. Internal CSS

Internal CSS is written inside the <style> tag in the <head> section of an HTML document.

Example
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Internal CSS</title>

    <style>
        h1 {
            color: blue;
        }

        p {
            font-size: 18px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Welcome</h1>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>

</body>
</html>
Syntax
selector {
    property: value;
}

Example:

h1 {
    color: red;
    font-size: 30px;
}

Use: Internal CSS is useful when styling a single HTML page.

4. External CSS

External CSS is written in a separate .css file.

For example:

project/
│
├── index.html
└── style.css
style.css
h1 {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 30px;
}

p {
    color: green;
}
index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>External CSS</title>

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Welcome</h1>
    <p>This is my website.</p>

</body>
</html>
Advantages
Easy to maintain
Same CSS can be used on multiple HTML pages
Keeps HTML and CSS separate
Recommended for real projects
5. CSS Properties

CSS properties are used to change the appearance and layout of HTML elements.

color

Changes the text color.

p {
    color: red;
}

You can also use:

p {
    color: #ff0000;
}
background-color

Changes the background color.

div {
    background-color: lightblue;
}
font-size

Changes the size of the text.

p {
    font-size: 20px;
}

Common units:

font-size: 16px;
font-size: 1rem;
font-size: 1.5em;
6. Margin

Margin creates space outside an element.

div {
    margin: 20px;
}
Individual sides
div {
    margin-top: 10px;
    margin-right: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    margin-left: 20px;
}
Short form
div {
    margin: 10px 20px;
}

Meaning:

10px  → Top & Bottom
20px  → Left & Right
7. Padding

Padding creates space inside an element, between the content and border.

div {
    padding: 20px;
}
Example
<div class="box">
    Hello Student
</div>
.box {
    background-color: lightblue;
    padding: 20px;
}
Margin vs Padding
        Margin
          ↓
    ┌───────────────┐
    │    Padding    │
    │   ┌───────┐   │
    │   │Content│   │
    │   └───────┘   │
    └───────────────┘

Remember:

Margin → Space outside
Padding → Space inside
8. Other Common CSS Properties
Property	Purpose	Example
color	Text color	color: red;
background-color	Background color	background-color: blue;
font-size	Text size	font-size: 20px;
font-family	Font style	font-family: Arial;
font-weight	Text thickness	font-weight: bold;
text-align	Text alignment	text-align: center;
margin	Outside spacing	margin: 20px;
padding	Inside spacing	padding: 20px;
width	Element width	width: 300px;
height	Element height	height: 200px;
border	Adds border	border: 1px solid black;
border-radius	Rounded corners	border-radius: 10px;
9. Complete Example
HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Student Card</title>

    <style>
        .student-card {
            background-color: lightblue;
            color: black;
            width: 300px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 30px;
            border: 2px solid blue;
            border-radius: 10px;
        }

        .student-name {
            color: blue;
            font-size: 25px;
        }

        .course {
            font-size: 18px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

    <div class="student-card">
        <h2 class="student-name">Rahul</h2>
        <p class="course">Java Full Stack Developer</p>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
